Correct the claim or use of sources, not just the neatness of the poster.","title":"Show the evidence","visual":[{"alt":"Atlas neighbours.","caption":"Cite atlas.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Fact strip</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">five places</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Risk</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">all called Asia</text></g></svg>"}]},{"check":{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Use an intentionally incorrect claim as a comparison.","options":["An answer copied without understanding","Use an intentionally incorrect claim as a comparison","A guess with no evidence","A claim unrelated to the source or place"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about neighbour with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nCalling every nearby place “Asia” without Pacific nations ignores the Pacific neighbourhood. Here is a more productive model: Example: using an atlas, students list nearby countries such as Indonesia, Papua New Guinea, New Zealand and Pacific island nations (as shown on their map). They note ocean distances, not land borders, for many links.\n\nTRY FIRST\nDescribe why the warned-against claim fails, then write a corrected claim with evidence.\n\nFEEDBACK\nAnswers depend on the atlas (e.g. Indonesia, PNG, New Zealand); water e.g. Timor Sea / Coral Sea / Tasman Sea as appropriate.","explain":"Use an intentionally incorrect claim as a comparison. Name its specific HSIE error, then show the corrected evidence-based claim.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner base claims on sources, places and respectful community knowledge, rather than guessing from a single picture. Keep every claim linked to evidence.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the sources, map features or community examples that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Compare your record with the feedback: did you use the same skill and show your evidence? If a detail is uncertain, identify what source you would need next. One deliberate, evidence-based revision is more useful than writing a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Changing the wording without repairing the evidence does not fix the error. Calling every nearby place “Asia” without Pacific nations ignores the Pacific neighbourhood. Correct the claim or use of sources, not just the neatness of the poster.","title":"Test a misconception"},{"check":{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Use the same HSIE idea on a new place, person, source or context, not a copy of the worked example.","options":["Use the same HSIE idea on a new place, person, source or context, not a copy of the worked example","A claim unrelated to the source or place","An answer copied without understanding","A guess with no evidence"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about neighbour with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nAustralia’s neighbours in Asia and the Pacific include countries and island nations across water borders. Neighbours are identified with atlases; relationships include trade, travel and shared environments. Now choose a different example to show you can transfer the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nInquire into or describe one fresh example that uses the same skill and write a short evidence-based reason.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe new example should use the same HSIE idea as the model, but contain different evidence.","explain":"Use the same HSIE idea on a new place, person, source or context, not a copy of the worked example. Review accuracy of evidence.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner base claims on sources, places and respectful community knowledge, rather than guessing from a single picture. Keep every claim linked to evidence.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the sources, map features or community examples that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Compare your record with the feedback: did you use the same skill and show your evidence? If a detail is uncertain, identify what source you would need next. One deliberate, evidence-based revision is more useful than writing a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Repeating the worked example cannot show transfer to new evidence. Correct the claim or use of sources, not just the neatness of the poster.","title":"Transfer to a new inquiry"}],"title":"Year 4, Week 12, Lesson 1: Australia and its neighbours in Asia and the Pacific","unit_number":3,"unit_title":"Australia and its neighbours","week":12,"week_in_unit":2,"word_challenges":null,"worked_example":"WORK THROUGH THIS\nExample: using an atlas, students list nearby countries such as Indonesia, Papua New Guinea, New Zealand and Pacific island nations (as shown on their map). They note ocean distances, not land borders, for many links.\n\nWrite what makes this example accurate, naming the sources, places or community details used.","worked_visuals":[{"alt":"Australia and neighbours.","caption":"Include Pacific nations.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Neighbours</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">Asia–Pacific</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Water links</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">not always land borders</text></g></svg>"},{"alt":"Atlas neighbours.","caption":"Cite atlas.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\